{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_logging","fact_refs":[0],"question":"Why shouldn't you put Django logging calls directly in settings.py?","answer":"Because Django's logging is configured as part of the setup() function, so at the point settings.py runs, logging has not been set up yet and the calls may not work as expected."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_logging","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What's the recommended way to obtain a logger instance in Django code, and what name should be passed to it?","answer":"Call logging.getLogger(__name__), passing __name__ so the logger is named after the current Python module's dotted path."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_logging","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What are the five logging severity levels Django mentions?","answer":"DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR, and CRITICAL."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_logging","fact_refs":[3],"question":"By default, will a DEBUG-level log message appear in the console under Django's default logging configuration?","answer":"No — records with a level lower than WARNING do not appear in the console by default; this requires additional configuration to change."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_logging","fact_refs":[5],"question":"In the example Django LOGGING configuration, what does the 'file' handler do?","answer":"It uses Python's FileHandler class to save log records of level DEBUG and higher to a file named general.log at the project root."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_logging","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What is the effect of setting disable_existing_loggers to False in a Django LOGGING dict?","answer":"It retains Django's existing default loggers so the custom configuration extends rather than replaces them."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_logging","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What does an unnamed ('') logger mapping do in a Django LOGGING configuration?","answer":"It processes log records from all loggers, since the empty string name is unnamed and matches every logger."}
